{#- One TV glyph for the whole feature. The marker in a card's meta row and
    the corner overlay on a 10-minute card both draw this same shape, so a
    recording reads as one glyph wherever it turns up.

    Drawn the way check.njk is: no width/height attributes, because every
    context sizes it in CSS, and stroke follows currentColor so the resting
    and dimmed states are just a colour on the parent. The stroke is thinner
    than the check mark's 2.25 - a screen with an antenna is more geometry
    than a single tick in the same 16x16 box, and anything heavier starts
    closing up the screen's corners at the 10-12px this actually renders at.

    Three things about the geometry were decided by looking at the rendered
    pixels rather than at the numbers, and are worth writing down because
    none of them is obvious from the path data.

    It is one path, not a <rect> plus a <polyline>. The ink tokens carry
    alpha - --ink-soft is rgba(230, 240, 255, 0.82) - so two separate
    elements whose strokes overlap composite that alpha twice. Where the
    antenna's apex met the screen's top edge that came out at luminance 230
    against the 209 the token asks for: a bright dot sitting exactly where
    the eye lands, which was most of what made the mark read as a badge.
    Stroking a single path unions the two subpaths first and composites the
    result once, so the whole glyph is the one grey it is meant to be.

    The ink sits high in the box, spanning y 1.25 to 12.75 of 16 rather than
    straddling the centre. In the meta row the glyph's box is centred by the
    flex row, but the time beside it is text: its digits sit above their line
    box's centre, because that line box also has to make room for descenders
    nothing in "09:00" actually uses. Ink centred in the box therefore lands
    about three quarters of a pixel below the digits it is standing next to.
    Drawing the glyph a viewBox unit high puts its optical centre on theirs.

    The antenna spreads to x 4.75 and 11.25 rather than hugging the middle.
    Only the part above the screen's top stroke survives at these sizes -
    a pixel and a half of each arm - so what makes the two arms read as a V
    rather than as two dots on a lump is how far apart their tips are, not
    how long the arms are. This is the spread Feather's tv icon uses, redrawn
    into the 16x16 box the rest of these glyphs live in.

    Set `tvClass` before including to hang a context class on it. #}
<svg class="tv{% if tvClass %} {{ tvClass }}{% endif %}" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 4.5H12A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 13.5 6V10.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 12 12H4A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 2.5 10.5V6A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 4 4.5ZM4.75 2 8 4.5 11.25 2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
